msgid "To call a service, use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:359
msgid "$ ros2 service call <service_name> <service_type> <arguments>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:363
msgid ""
"The ``<arguments>`` part is optional. For example, ``Empty`` typed services "
"don't have any arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:366
msgid "$ ros2 service call /clear std_srvs/srv/Empty"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:370
msgid "This clears the Turtlesim window of any lines drawn by the turtle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:374
msgid ""
"Next, spawn a new turtle by calling ``/spawn`` with arguments. Arguments in "
"a service call from the command line must be in YAML syntax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:377
msgid "To spawn a new turtle, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:379
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service call /spawn turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn \"{x: 2, y: 2, theta: "
"0.2, name: ''}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:384
msgid ""
"On distributions older than Kilted, use the type ``turtlesim/srv/Spawn`` "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:386
msgid "The terminal shows the request that was sent and the service response:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:388
msgid ""
"requester: making request: turtlesim_msgs.srv.Spawn_Request(x=2.0, y=2.0, "
"theta=0.2, name='')\n"
"\n"
"response:\n"
"turtlesim_msgs.srv.Spawn_Response(name='turtle2')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:395
msgid "The Turtlesim window updates with the newly spawned turtle:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:400
msgid ""
"8 Monitor the communication between a service client and a service server"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:402
msgid ""
"Sometimes you may need to watch the request and response traffic as it "
"happens. For example, you might want to debug a client that seems to hang, "
"or verify that a server received the fields you expect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:405
msgid "Use ``ros2 service echo`` to print that live traffic:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:407
msgid "$ ros2 service echo <service_name | service_type> <arguments>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:411
msgid ""
"``ros2 service echo`` needs service introspection, which Turtlesim does not "
"enable. Use the ``demo_nodes_cpp`` introspection demo instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:414
msgid ""
"Start the introspection demo, which runs the ``introspection_client`` and "
"``introspection_service`` nodes, and leave this terminal running:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:416
msgid "$ ros2 launch demo_nodes_cpp introspect_services_launch.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:420
msgid ""
"``ros2 service echo`` only works when service introspection is enabled. In "
"your own nodes, you would call ``configure_introspection`` on the client and "
"server after you create them. The demo exposes that setting as parameters, "
"so you can turn introspection on from the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:424
msgid "Open another terminal and enable introspection on both demo nodes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:426
msgid ""
"$ ros2 param set /introspection_service service_configure_introspection "
"contents\n"
"$ ros2 param set /introspection_client client_configure_introspection "
"contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:431
msgid ""
"Open another terminal and watch the ``/add_two_ints`` service. The demo "
"client calls that service repeatedly, so you should see a continuous stream "
